<!-- overview -->

kubeadm does not support automated ways of change the IP address of a control plane node,
worker node, or the IP address of a load balancer that balances the deployed
kube-apiservers on control plane nodes. At the same time, kubeadm does hardcode such
IP addresses in a number of locations.

This guide shows the correct sequence of steps that need to be performed
to achieve the reconfiguration of such IP addresses.

{{< warning >}}
This guide includes sensitive steps that may introduce prologued cluster downtime
if not executed correctly. In some situations minimal downtime may not be avoidable.
Updating IP addresses of the cluster load balancer or control plane nodes is not a recommended
action and should be avoided if possible.
{{< /warning >}}

## {{% heading "prerequisites" %}}

- You need a cluster that was deployed using kubeadm.
- Have a text editor installed on all hosts and experted as `KUBE_EDITOR`.
- Have terminal connectivity to all hosts in the cluster with SSH or another method.
- Get familiar with kubeadm's
[Implementation details](/docs/reference/setup-tools/kubeadm/implementation-details).
- Get familiar with
[Reconfiguring a kubeadm cluster](/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/kubeadm/kubeadm-reconfigure).
- Get familiar with
[Options for Highly Available Topology](/docs/setup/production-environment/tools/kubeadm/ha-topology).

